00:21:53.840 --> 00:21:57.423
including biology,
chemistry, physics, Econ,

00:21:57.423 --> 00:22:00.370
APCs principles, you will see

00:22:00.370 --> 00:22:01.220
there's a view

00:22:01.220 --> 00:22:03.070
where you have videos on the left.

00:22:03.070 --> 00:22:05.250
And on the right, you have practice.

00:22:05.250 --> 00:22:06.900
And if you click on that,

00:22:06.900 --> 00:22:08.540
you'll see that as you get things right,

00:22:08.540 --> 00:22:11.145
it has these, what we
call mastery mechanics.

00:22:11.145 --> 00:22:15.240
So as you can see right here on the,

00:22:15.240 --> 00:22:17.470
this is showing the various lessons,

00:22:17.470 --> 00:22:20.040
but you can see these
mastery points available.

00:22:20.040 --> 00:22:22.660
And then if you were to click
on one of those lessons,

00:22:22.660 --> 00:22:23.610
then you'll be able to see

00:22:23.610 --> 00:22:26.639
the various exercises you can do,

00:22:26.639 --> 00:22:27.840
and get feedback.

00:22:27.840 --> 00:22:28.730
And there's different levels,

00:22:28.730 --> 00:22:30.350
you could do it at the skill level,

00:22:30.350 --> 00:22:33.275
you can do it at a quiz level,

00:22:33.275 --> 00:22:34.460
a unit test level,

00:22:34.460 --> 00:22:37.080
a mastery challenge level
or a course challenge.

00:22:37.080 --> 00:22:40.930
So I encourage you to go to
our site and try it all out.

00:22:40.930 --> 00:22:45.930
All right, so other questions.

00:22:45.980 --> 00:22:49.410
So some of these are a
little bit more general.

00:22:49.410 --> 00:22:51.500
So from YouTube, Michael Azhar says,

00:22:51.500 --> 00:22:54.280
'Sal, is there any way
I can improve my skills

00:22:54.280 --> 00:22:56.370
using Khan Academy efficiently?'

00:22:56.370 --> 00:22:58.130
So I have some ideas and
actually I'm curious Tim,

00:22:58.130 --> 00:22:59.700
what you think about this.

00:22:59.700 --> 00:23:02.630
So I think the best way to
efficiently use Khan Academy

00:23:02.630 --> 00:23:04.840
is analogous to what
you just heard Tim say,

00:23:04.840 --> 00:23:06.650
that he coaches his students to do.

00:23:06.650 --> 00:23:09.500
Is start at a level that's maybe earlier

00:23:09.500 --> 00:23:10.480
than your grade level,

00:23:10.480 --> 00:23:12.360
unless you're very,
very confident in math.

00:23:12.360 --> 00:23:13.193
But if you're not,

00:23:13.193 --> 00:23:14.720
starting at an earlier grade level,

00:23:14.720 --> 00:23:16.540
and I would start with
the course challenges,

00:23:16.540 --> 00:23:18.860
because that samples
everything in the class,

00:23:18.860 --> 00:23:19.693
and if you're able

00:23:19.693 --> 00:23:21.550
to get through the course
challenges quite easily,

00:23:21.550 --> 00:23:23.250
you can move on to the next grade level.

00:23:23.250 --> 00:23:24.083
But if you're not able to

00:23:24.083 --> 00:23:25.620
move through the course challenges easily,

00:23:25.620 --> 00:23:27.020
the things that you got wrong,

00:23:27.020 --> 00:23:28.235
you can then go to those units,

00:23:28.235 --> 00:23:30.460
and then try to get
mastery in those units.

00:23:30.460 --> 00:23:33.450
And I would say that's the most
efficient way to get mastery

00:23:33.450 --> 00:23:35.478
at some of your foundational subjects,

00:23:35.478 --> 00:23:37.560
as quickly as possible.

00:23:37.560 --> 00:23:39.270
There's also ways you know,

00:23:39.270 --> 00:23:40.290
if you're gonna learn anything,

00:23:40.290 --> 00:23:44.630
whether it's math or piano
or improve your free throw,

00:23:44.630 --> 00:23:46.500
it's all about consistency.

00:23:46.500 --> 00:23:49.440
A lot of times we're all guilty of binge,

00:23:49.440 --> 00:23:51.780
you know, studying or binge exercising

00:23:51.780 --> 00:23:53.370
then we do nothing for a few weeks.

00:23:53.370 --> 00:23:56.870
It's much better if you can
do a solid 30 minutes a day,

00:23:56.870 --> 00:23:57.830
40 minutes a day,

00:23:57.830 --> 00:23:59.970
if you're younger maybe 20 minutes a day.

00:23:59.970 --> 00:24:02.240
You're going to see that if
you do that consistently,

00:24:02.240 --> 00:24:04.920
that's going, you're gonna notice

00:24:04.920 --> 00:24:06.690
you performing better,

00:24:06.690 --> 00:24:08.280
you're looking at the world differently,

00:24:08.280 --> 00:24:10.730
in a matter of a month or two.

00:24:10.730 --> 00:24:12.973
Tim, anything to add to that?

00:24:13.820 --> 00:24:15.980
- Yeah, I can't tell
you how powerful it is

00:24:15.980 --> 00:24:18.920
to use the early math course to focus

00:24:18.920 --> 00:24:22.170
on mastering the numbers sense concepts,

00:24:22.170 --> 00:24:24.320
which is usually, research has shown,

00:24:24.320 --> 00:24:26.810
is what holds kids and even adults back,

00:24:26.810 --> 00:24:29.640
is grasping the true meaning of numbers,

00:24:29.640 --> 00:24:31.720
and place value and fractions.

00:24:31.720 --> 00:24:33.060
So early math,

00:24:33.060 --> 00:24:36.004
and then the arithmetic
course on Khan Academy,

00:24:36.004 --> 00:24:39.140
is a good summary review of everything,

00:24:39.140 --> 00:24:43.730
basically K through maybe seventh grade,

00:24:43.730 --> 00:24:44.940
those are great.

00:24:44.940 --> 00:24:47.360
Turbo super fast reviews
to get through everything

00:24:47.360 --> 00:24:49.350
and make sure you truly understand

00:24:49.350 --> 00:24:51.910
what you need for junior
high and high school.

00:24:51.910 --> 00:24:53.170
Then for older students,

00:24:53.170 --> 00:24:54.990
the pre-algebra course really teaches

00:24:54.990 --> 00:24:58.220
those basic skills of
working the key concepts

00:24:58.220 --> 00:24:59.943
of algebraic manipulation.

00:25:01.020 --> 00:25:02.880
- And one thing I'll add, you know,

00:25:02.880 --> 00:25:04.110
both Tim and I have been talking a lot

00:25:04.110 --> 00:25:06.160
about this importance of
really strong foundations.

00:25:06.160 --> 00:25:07.660
But I've seen a lot of parents

00:25:07.660 --> 00:25:10.080
have a little bit of anxiety
when it comes to math.

00:25:10.080 --> 00:25:12.350
And I think this is also an opportunity,

00:25:12.350 --> 00:25:14.960
now that we're all
socially distanced at home,

00:25:14.960 --> 00:25:16.530
that if you do have some time,

00:25:16.530 --> 00:25:18.790
and maybe even do it next to your child,

00:25:18.790 --> 00:25:20.540
it's really great modeling for them.

00:25:20.540 --> 00:25:22.800
For you to do what Tim has just mentioned,

00:25:22.800 --> 00:25:23.720
go back to early learning,

00:25:23.720 --> 00:25:24.740
if you know that material,

00:25:24.740 --> 00:25:26.010
you'll get through it quite fast.

00:25:26.010 --> 00:25:27.500
And if it takes you a
little bit more time,

00:25:27.500 --> 00:25:28.860
it's because you needed it.

00:25:28.860 --> 00:25:30.950
And I think that'll
change your perspective.

00:25:30.950 --> 00:25:34.423
And it's very powerful
modeling for the students.

00:25:35.650 --> 00:25:37.770
- Yeah, I have had a couple of parents

00:25:37.770 --> 00:25:39.007
do that with their children.

00:25:39.007 --> 00:25:42.890
And not only does it help the
parent, it encourages them.

00:25:46.124 --> 00:25:48.563
This year, who was scared of fractions.

00:25:50.790 --> 00:25:52.700
Her mom has been working
together with her,

00:25:52.700 --> 00:25:54.160
on her own account of Khan,

00:25:54.160 --> 00:25:55.700
and the two of them together are acing

00:25:55.700 --> 00:25:59.170
our state practice tests
that we have been taking.

00:25:59.170 --> 00:26:00.950
So it's been great,

00:26:00.950 --> 00:26:02.473
parents, motivate your kids.

00:26:04.060 --> 00:26:05.280
- That's awesome.

00:26:05.280 --> 00:26:06.610
Let's see, there's a
question from Facebook,

00:26:06.610 --> 00:26:07.443
Edward Swan says,

00:26:07.443 --> 00:26:10.410
'Are there plans to match up
